{"step_index":218,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:02:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:02:33+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:02:33+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/theorem_prover.py`\nTotal Lines: 890\nTotal Bytes: 40615\nShowing lines 200 to 300\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n200: \n201: \n202: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n203: # DeepSeek-Prover-V2 stub\n204: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n205: \n206: class DeepSeekProverV2(BaseProver):\n207:     \"\"\"\n208:     Stub for DeepSeek-Prover-V2.\n209: \n210:     Reference: Xin et al. \"DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Advancing Formal Mathematical\n211:     Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning for Subgoal Decomposition\" (2024).\n212:     Uses RL-trained subgoal decomposition to construct step-by-step Lean 4 proofs.\n213: \n214:     The prover decomposes the goal into subgoals, solves each independently,\n215:     and assembles a complete proof.  This architecture is particularly effective\n216:     for multi-step graph-theory lemmas.\n217: \n218:     To enable: set cfg.prover_api_url to a DeepSeek-Prover-V2 endpoint.\n219:     \"\"\"\n220: \n221:     name = \"deepseek-prover-v2\"\n222: \n223:     _ENDPOINT_PATH = \"/v1/lean4/prove\"\n224:     _EXPECTED_REQUEST = {\n225:         \"statement\": \"<lean4 theorem string>\",\n226:         \"context\": \"import Mathlib\",\n227:         \"strategy\": \"subgoal_decomposition\",   # or \"best_first_search\"\n228:         \"max_subgoals\": 10,\n229:         \"timeout_s\": 120,\n230:     }\n231: \n232:     def __init__(self, cfg: Config = CONFIG):\n233:         self.cfg = cfg\n234: \n235:     def prove(self, conjecture: Conjecture) -> ProverResponse:\n236:         if conjecture.lean_statement is None:\n237:             return ProverResponse(\n238:                 success=False, error=\"No Lean 4 statement available\", model_name=self.name\n239:             )\n240:         if not self.cfg.prover_api_url:\n241:             return ProverResponse(\n242:                 success=False, _stub=True, model_name=self.name,\n243:                 error=\"DeepSeek-Prover-V2 API not configured \"\n244:                       \"(set cfg.prover_api_url to a Colab/GPU endpoint — see colab/)\",\n245:             )\n246:         # Real, kernel-verified HTTP call via the generic endpoint client.\n247:         client = LocalEndpointProver(self.cfg, name=self.name,\n248:                                      endpoint_path=self._ENDPOINT_PATH)\n249:         return client.prove(conjecture)\n250: \n251: \n252: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n253: # Lean subprocess prover (local, works for trivial statements)\n254: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n255: \n256: class LeanSubprocessProver(BaseProver):\n257:     \"\"\"\n258:     Attempt to find a proof using the local `lean` binary + simple tactics:\n259:       - `decide`     (works for decidable propositions on finite types)\n260:       - `simp`\n261:       - `norm_num`\n262:       - `omega`      (linear arithmetic over integers/naturals)\n263: \n264:     This is NOT a neural prover.  It can close a small set of goals that\n265:     reduce to arithmetic or decidable search, making it useful for sanity-\n266:     checking that the Lean 4 statement compiles and for trivial conjectures.\n267:     \"\"\"\n268: \n269:     name = \"lean-subprocess\"\n270: \n271:     _SIMPLE_TACTICS = [\"decide\", \"simp\", \"norm_num\", \"omega\", \"ring\", \"tauto\"]\n272: \n273:     def __init__(self, cfg: Config = CONFIG):\n274:         self.cfg = cfg\n275:         self._available = bool(shutil.which(cfg.lean_binary))\n276: \n277:     def prove(self, conjecture: Conjecture) -> ProverResponse:\n278:         if not self._available:\n279:             return ProverResponse(\n280:                 success=False,\n281:                 error=f\"Lean binary not found: {self.cfg.lean_binary}\",\n282:                 model_name=self.name,\n283:             )\n284:         if conjecture.lean_statement is None:\n285:             return ProverResponse(success=False, error=\"No statement\", model_name=self.name)\n286: \n287:         t0 = time.time()\n288:         for tactic in self._SIMPLE_TACTICS:\n289:             proof = self._try_tactic(conjecture.lean_statement, tactic)\n290:             if proof:\n291:                 elapsed = time.time() - t0\n292:                 logger.info(\n293:                     \"[LeanSubprocess] Proved %s with tactic `%s`\", conjecture.id, tactic\n294:                 )\n295:                 return ProverResponse(\n296:                     success=True,\n297:                     proof_tactics=proof,\n298:                     model_name=self.name,\n299:                     elapsed_s=elapsed,\n300:                 )\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":219,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:02:33Z","thinking":"**Evaluating Candidate Proposition**\n\nInvestigating the `Candidate` proposition to determine provability. Considering a proof attempt using helper lemmas to establish `prove_e20263a9 : Candidate`. Initial assessment involves examining the structure of `Candidate` to determine the necessary helper lemmas.\n\n\n**Analyzing Roman Domination Number**\n\nThe goal is to show the radius plus one is less than or equal to the Roman domination number.  The Roman domination number is the minimum Roman weight over all Roman dominating functions. A constant function with value 2 is a Roman dominating function, proving the non-emptiness of the set from which the minimum is taken. The properties of the minimum and how it is obtained is crucial.\n\n\n**Deriving Graph Radius Bound**\n\nRadius G is defined as the minimum eccentricity over all vertices, ensuring radius is less than or equal to eccentricity for any vertex v. If a vertex's eccentricity plus one is less than or equal to the Roman domination number, then radius plus one is also less than or equal to the Roman domination number.  Eccentricity can be defined in terms of `ballWithin` function and `minUpTo`.  `ballWithin` characterizes the set of vertices within a certain distance.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh Challenge_e20263a9.lean","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Verify check.sh","toolSummary":"Run check.sh"}}]}
